  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1237 PM CST WED NOV 4 2026\n\nThis afternoon, a surface low will move out of Iowa and Wisconsin\ntoward Upper Michigan by Thursday, dragging a cold front across\nmuch of the Gulf Coast States, including Central Alabama. Shower\nactivity will become widespread ahead of this front, along with a\nfew thunderstorms supported by frontal forcing. Latest guidance\ncontinues to redevelop activity right along the cold front this\nafternoon into tonight, which presents the severe potential\nacross the forecast area. Looking at current conditions, we are\nseeing slightly more vertical cloud development along the line in\nWest Alabama, though no lightning has been observed yet. Dew\npoints in the low 70s and partial clearing are yielding decent\nCAPE values, albeit with a skinny CAPE profile, and sounding data\nindicates a persistent warm layer aloft. While modest deep-layer\nshear is present, low-level shear/SRH remains weak. Based off a\nspecial 18z sounding\n\nBy Thursday, the system will continue to progress through the SE\nportions of the CWA with all precipitation expected to exit\nCentral Alabama by late Thursday evening. Behind the front on\nFriday, a much colder Canadian ridge will build to lower the\ntemperatures back into the 40s (and maybe some mid to upper 30s)\nfor lows across Central Alabama for this weekend into the first\npart of next week. Guidance is trending colder behind this front\nwith each successive run. There is a medium chance that we could\nsee our first frost as early as Saturday night/Sunday morning\nnow. This would be the first of multiple days where portions of\nthe area may see frost and even freezing temperatures in the\nnormally cooler locations. .\n\n16 and 32/JDavis",
